"[2603.04909] Hat guessing with proper colorings (https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.04909)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: last month; [Submitted on 5 Mar 2026 (v1), last revised 24 Aug 2026 (this version, v4)] ... We derive general upper bounds in terms of the number of vertices, chromatic number, and maximum degree, and obtain improved bounds for book graphs. ... UPDATE: We added Theorem 1.3, which gives bounds the proper coloring hat guessing number by a multiple of the maximum degree.\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 5 Mar 2026 (v1), last revised 24 Aug 2026 (this version, v4)]\n\n# Title:Hat guessing with proper colorings\n\nAuthors:Sam Adriaensen, Peter Bentley, Anurag Bishnoi, Wouter Cames van Batenburg, Michael Kreiger, Lars van der Kuil, Saptarshi Mandal, Anurag Ramachandran, James Tuite\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Hat guessing with proper colorings, by Sam Adriaensen and 8 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:We initiate the study of the hat guessing number of a graph where the adversary is only allowed to provide a proper coloring of the graph. This is the largest number q$q$ for which there is a guessing strategy on each vertex that only depends on its neighborhood, such that for every proper coloring of the graph with q$q$ colors at least one vertex guesses its color correctly. In this variation, we prove that the hat guessing number of the complete graphs on n$n$ vertices is 2 n−1$2n - 1$, which is roughly twice the classical hat guessing number of the complete graph. Our winning strategy is related to finding perfect matchings between the middle layers of the boolean poset of dimension 2 n−1$2n - 1$. We prove that the hat guessing number of all trees on n≥3$n \\geq 3$ vertices is equal to 4$4$. We derive general upper bounds in terms of the number of vertices, chromatic number, and maximum degree, and obtain improved bounds for book graphs. Using our results and an ILP formulation of the problem, we determine the exact hat guessing number for all graphs on at most 4$4$ vertices, give bounds on graphs on 5$5$ vertices, and suggest some open problems.\n\nComments:  | 12 pages. UPDATE: We added Theorem 1.3, which gives bounds the proper coloring hat guessing number by a multiple of the maximum degree. Extra Update: we updated the Acknowledgements\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2603.04909 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2603.04909v4 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.04909 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Sam Adriaensen [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 5 Mar 2026 07:52:36 UTC (13 KB)\n[v2] Mon, 8 Jun 2026 14:34:47 UTC (13 KB)\n[v3] Thu, 23 Jul 2026 09:03:59 UTC (16 KB)\n[v4] Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:36:42 UTC (16 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Hat guessing with proper colorings, by Sam Adriaensen and 8 other authors\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source\n\nview license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeedback Game on Eulerian Graphs: Discrete and Computational Geometry, Graphs, and Games | Thai Journal of Mathematics (https://thaijmath.com/index.php/thaijmath/article/view/1542)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; In the latter half of the paper, we discuss the feedback game on two subclasses of Eulerian graphs, i.e., triangular grid graphs and toroidal grid graphs.\n\n# Feedback Game on Eulerian Graphs\n\n## Discrete and Computational Geometry, Graphs, and Games\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Naoki Matsumoto\n  * Atsuki Nagao\n\n## Keywords:\n\nfeedback game, edge geography, Eulerian graph, triangular grid graph, Toroidal grid graph\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we introduce a two-player impartial game on graphs, called feedback game, which is a variant of generalized geography. Feedback game can be regarded as undirected edge geography with an additional rule that the first player who goes back to the starting vertex wins the game. We consider feedback game on an Eulerian graph since the game ends only by going back to the starting vertex. We first show that it is PSPACE-complete in general to determine the winner of the feedback game on Eulerian graphs even if its maximum degree is at most~4. In the latter half of the paper, we discuss the feedback game on two subclasses of Eulerian graphs, i.e., triangular grid graphs and toroidal grid graphs.\n\n## References\n\n## Downloads\n\n  * PDF\n\n## Published\n\n2023-12-31\n\n## Issue\n\nVol. 21 No. 4 (2023): December\n\n## Section\n\nArticles\n\n## How to Cite\n\nFeedback Game on Eulerian Graphs: Discrete and Computational Geometry, Graphs, and Games. (2023). Thai Journal of Mathematics, 21(4), 751–768. https://thaijmath.com/index.php/thaijmath/article/view/1542--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nN. Matsumoto et al.  Feedback game on Eulerian graphs  TJCDCGG2021 (https://www.math.science.cmu.ac.th/tjcdcggg/Book-abstract.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; In this paper, we investigate feedback game on Eulerian graphs. ... The conjecture still unsolved. ... If \\(n \\neq 2^m - 3\\) with \\(m \\ge 2\\), then Bob wins the game on the triangular grid graph \\(T_n\\) with a starting vertex \\(v_0^0\\).\nN. Matsumoto et al.  Feedback game on Eulerian graphs  TJCDCGG2021\n\nIn this paper, we investigate feedback game on Eulerian graphs. Note that if a given connected graph \\(G\\) is Eulerian, then the game does not end until the token goes back to the starting vertex \\(s\\), and further observe that Bob always wins feedback game on any connected bipartite Eulerian graph (cf. [10]): Let \\(G\\) be a connected bipartite Eulerian graph, and so, all vertices of \\(G\\) are properly colored by two colors, black and white. Without loss of generality, we may suppose that the starting vertex is colored by black. Throughout the game on \\(G\\), a token is always moved to a white (resp., black) vertex by Alice (resp., Bob). Thus Bob necessarily wins the game.\n\n## 2 Main results\n\nHere main results are in this paper. Though definitions of technical terms are omitted, one can see them on ArXiV version*. The conjecture still unsolved. We hope one enjoy solving this open problem.\n\nTheorem 2.1. It is PSPACE-complete to determine whether there exists a winning strategy for the first player in feedback game, even if the given graph is Eulerian.\n\nTheorem 2.2. If \\(n \\neq 2^m - 3\\) with \\(m \\ge 2\\), then Bob wins the game on the triangular grid graph \\(T_n\\) with a starting vertex \\(v_0^0\\).\n\nLemma 2.3. There is no even kernel graph of \\(T_n\\) that does not include \\(v_0^0\\) when \\(n > 1\\).\n\nTheorem 2.4. Alice wins feedback game on \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_5\\) with a starting vertex \\(v_0^0\\).\n\nTheorem 2.5. If \\(n = 2^m - 3\\) with \\(m \\ge 2\\), then the triangular grid graph \\(T_n\\) has no even kernel graph.\n\nTheorem 2.6. If \\(\\gcd(m,n)=c>1\\), then Bob can win feedback game on \\(m \\times n\\) toroidal grid graph.\n\nTheorem 2.7. If \\(m=2\\) or \\(m=3\\), and \\(\\gcd(m,n)=1\\), then Alice can win feedback game on \\(m \\times n\\) toroidal grid graph.\n\nTheorem 2.8. If \\(\\gcd(m,n)=1\\), then there exists no even kernel graph of \\(m \\times n\\) toroidal grid graph.\n\nConjecture 2.9. Alice can win feedback game on \\(m \\times n\\) toroidal grid graph. if \\(\\gcd(m,n)=1\\).\n\n## References\n\n[1] B. Allen and M.A. Nowak, Games on graphs, *Eur. Math. Soc.* **1** (2014), 113–151.  \n[2] C. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Thomas in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AThomas+in%3Acs)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Nov 04 2026 math.CO cs.DM arXiv:2611.04577v1 ... This answers a question of Alon et al., disproves the conjecture $\\textrm{HG}(G) \\le 2\\Delta(G)$ of Bosek et al., and improves on the construction of Latyshev and Kokhas, which achieves $\\textrm{HG}(G) = 4\\Delta(G)/3$. The hat guessing number thus provides a natural combinatorial problem in which the Lovász Local Lemma is asymptotically tight.\nNov 04 2026 math.CO cs.DM arXiv:2611.04577v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2611.04577, author = {Thomas Delépine and Matthieu Rosenfeld}, title = {{T}he {L}ovász {L}ocal {L}emma {I}s {S}harp for the {H}at {G}uessing {N}umber}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2611.04577}, note = {arXiv:2611.04577v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn the hat guessing game on a graph $G$, each vertex is occupied by a player wearing a hat whose color is chosen from a set of $q$ colors. Each player knows the graph and their position in it and sees only the hat colors of their neighbors. The players may agree on a strategy beforehand but must simultaneously try to guess their own hat colors without communicating. They win if at least one player guesses their own hat color correctly. The hat guessing number of $G$, denoted by $\\textrm{HG}(G)$, is the largest integer $q$ for which the players have a strategy that guarantees a win for every assignment of hat colors. A direct application of the symmetric Lovász Local Lemma shows that $\\textrm{HG}(G) < e\\Delta$ for every graph $G$ of maximum degree at most $\\Delta \\ge 1$. We prove that this bound is asymptotically sharp: for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ and every sufficiently large $\\Delta$, there exists a graph $G$ of maximum degree at most $\\Delta$ with $\\textrm{HG}(G) \\ge (e - \\varepsilon)\\Delta$. This answers a question of Alon et al., disproves the conjecture $\\textrm{HG}(G) \\le 2\\Delta(G)$ of Bosek et al., and improves on the construction of Latyshev and Kokhas, which achieves $\\textrm{HG}(G) = 4\\Delta(G)/3$. The hat guessing number thus provides a natural combinatorial problem in which the Lovász Local Lemma is asymptotically tight.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolitechnika Warszawska (https://repo.pw.edu.pl/docstore/download/WUTc80a76f2519b48a1b93d614f0ba61e66/WUTc7dbfb313eb84a468f15f168fe4d3ef9.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Bosek, A. ... Discrete Mathematics 344 (2021) 112620 ... Finally, we pose a conjecture relating the hat chromatic number and the traditional chromatic number of a graph. ... to the following conjecture, which is known to be true for the chromatic number χ(G) by the famous result of Erd ˝os [8]. ... Bradshaw, On the hat guessing number of a planar graph class, arXiv:2106 .01480v1 [math .\nB. Bosek, A. Dudek, M. Farnik et al.\nDiscrete Mathematics 344 (2021) 112620\nhope that some existing reﬁnements of the local lemma combined with algebraic techniques could be useful here. Actually, \nit may occur quite challenging to prove that HG(G) ⩽(e −ϵ)\u0002 for some ϵ > 0 and suﬃciently large \u0002.\nAnother possible relation between the hat chromatic number and vertex degrees is suggested below.\nConjecture 4. If G is a graph with degree sequence (d1, d2, ..., dn), then the sequence (d1, d2, ..., dn) is winning for G.\nThis is true when di ⩽2. Notice that for every cycle Cn the sequence (4, 3, ..., 3) is loosing, and for some cycles even \n(3, 3, ..., 3) is loosing. On the other hand, it is not hard to demonstrate that for every cycle the sequence (3, 2, 3, 4, 4, ..., 4)\nis winning.\nFinally, we pose a conjecture relating the hat chromatic number and the traditional chromatic number of a graph.\nConjecture 5. Every graph G satisﬁes\nχ(G) ⩽HG(G) + 1.\nThis supposition can be heuristically explained as follows. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics: AI research tracker · AI Proof Watch (https://aiproofwatch.com/problems/combinatorics?page=5)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterda