 Graphs\n\nMason Shurman, Scott Albert Sibley\n\nJuly 08, 2026\n\n* * *\n\n#### Abstract\n\nThe hat guessing number \\(HG(G)\\) of a graph is defined by the following game: each player is placed on a vertex and assigned a hat with one of \\(k\\) colors. Each vertex can see only the hat color of the other vertices it is connected to in \\(G\\). All vertices guess, simultaneously, the color of their own hat. The hat guessing number \\(HG(G)\\) is the largest \\(k\\) such that the players can guarantee that at least one of them guesses correctly. In this paper, we show a general bound on the hat guessing number of a graph \\(G\\) as a function of its order \\(n\\) and its maximum degree \\(\\Delta\\). This is the first nontrivial upper bound on \\(HG(G)\\) as a function of \\(\\Delta\\) and \\(n\\) when \\(\\Delta \\geq \\frac{n}{e}\\). From this result we also obtain that the hat guessing number of the random graph \\(G_{n,1/2}\\) is at most asymptotically \\(cn\\) for \\(c\\sim 0.809\\), and that graphs with maximum degrees of \\((1-\\varepsilon )n\\) for fixed \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) cannot have \\(HG(G)=(1-o(1))n\\).\nOur main result is the first such upper bound.\n\nTheorem 1. For any graph \\(G\\) on \\(n\\) vertices with maximum degree \\(\\Delta\\), \\[HG(G) \\leq 1+\\frac{\\Delta}{2}+ \\frac{\\sqrt{(\\Delta +2)^2 + 2(n^2-3n-n\\Delta)}}{2}.\\]\n\nOne of the most interesting applications of our result is to the random graph \\(G_{n,1/2}\\). Note that the maximum degree of \\(G_{n,1/2}\\) is \\((1+o(1))n/2\\geq n/e\\), so the Lovász Local Lemma argument does not yield a nontrivial result. In 2019, Bosek et al.in [2] showed that with high probability, that is, with probability tending to 1 as \\(n\\) tends to infinity, \\[(2+o(1))\\log_2(n)\\leq HG(G_{n,1/2})\\leq n-(1+o(1))\\log_2 n.\\] In 2020, Alon and Chizewer in [8] showed that with high probability, \\[HG(G_{n,1/2}) \\geq n^{1-o(1)}\\] by finding a subgraph with large hat guessing number. Using our main result, we get a better upper bound on \\(HG(G_{n,1/2})\\).\n\nCorollary 1. With high probability, \\[HG(G_{n,1/2})\\leq (1+o(1)) \\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{4}\\right)n \\sim 0.809n.\\]\n\nIt may be initially unclear whether the inequality in Theorem 1 is always better than the trivial bound of \\(n\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeedback game on 3-chromatic Eulerian triangulations of surfaces - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X24000052)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In the next section, we recall an important concept for the feedback game, called an even kernel (Definition 2.1), which is a good structure for Bob to win the game, and we prove Theorem 1.2 and Proposition 2.4.\nThe feedback game on the graph {E}_{n}${E}_{n}$ with its starting vertex {u}_{p},{v}_{p}${u}_{p},{v}_{p}$ or {w}_{p}${w}_{p}$ is denoted by E(n,p)$E(n,p)$, where n\\ge 1$n\\ge 1$ and 0\\le p\\le n$0\\le p\\le n$. Notice that the choice of {u}_{p},{v}_{p},{w}_{p}${u}_{p},{v}_{p},{w}_{p}$ does not matter due to symmetry. We give the complete characterization whether Alice or Bob wins the game on E(n,p)$E(n,p)$:\n\nTheorem 1.5\n\nFor any integers n$n$ and p$p$ with n\\ge 1$n\\ge 1$ and 0\\le p\\le n$0\\le p\\le n$, the winner of the game E(n,p)$E(n,p)$ is determined as follows:\n\nHere, “x\\equiv y$x\\equiv y$” means that “congruent modulo 3$3$”.\n\nIn the next section, we recall an important concept for the feedback game, called an even kernel (Definition 2.1), which is a good structure for Bob to win the game, and we prove Theorem 1.2 and Proposition 2.4. In Section 3, we introduce a local even kernel (Definition 3.1), which is a good local structure for Alice to win the game. In Section 4 (resp. 5), we shall give a proof of Theorem 1.5 (resp. 1.4).\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers 